> Module Qualifying_child

> Using Filers as F

# Qualifying child: the five tests of IRC 152(c), one person at a time

Direct File ("DF" below) facts `/familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild` and
its sub-tests in `familyAndHousehold.xml` (lines 758-845, 1585-1648,
1804-1885, 2009-2147, 2687-2705, 4731-4749) and `/familyAndHousehold/*/qcRelationshipTest`
in `dependentsRelationship.xml` (lines 986-1108), under
`../direct-file/direct-file/backend/src/main/resources/tax/`. Issue #16,
scope.md section 5.2. Tests: `catala/tests/test_qualifying_child.catala_en`.
Oracle comparison: `difftest/mappings/qualifying_child.mjs` against the
per-person oracle `oracle/persons.csv` (`make difftest-persons`).

DF keeps the people of the household in the collection `/familyAndHousehold`.
Every fact below is a per-person fact `/familyAndHousehold/*/x`. Following
`catala/CONVENTIONS.md` section 2, the collection is a `list of Person`, the
per-person facts are computed by the scope `QualifyingChildTest` applied to
one person, and `QualifyingChildren` maps it over the list.

The only fact read from outside the person is `/minimumFilerDateOfBirth`
(`filers.xml` line 3306), the date of birth of the oldest filer: the older of
the two filers if the return is treated as joint, else the primary filer. It
is the output `minimum_filer_date_of_birth` of `Filers.Filers` (issue #15),
and an input here.

The inputs are complete (CONVENTIONS section 3). DF asks some of them only
under a UI condition (the student question only between ages 19 and 23, the
disability question only when `disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits`, the
joint-return questions only of a married person, the support question only
after the relationship, age and residency tests pass). They are still inputs
here, and the law decides how they count: where DF's gate disagrees with the
statute, the statute wins (CONVENTIONS section 8, `# DF-BUG(n):` comments;
one case, the disability answer in the age test).

The tie-breakers of IRC 152(c)(4) cannot be computed from the return; a
person who needs them makes the return unsupported (DF-BUG-3, below).

Not here, on purpose: the qualifying relative and the dependent tests
(issue #17), the
children of divorced or separated parents (IRC 152(e), `dependentsBenefitSplit.xml`,
issue #18), and the credit-specific person tests (EITC, CTC, CDCC; issues
#26-#28). This module feeds those modules; it feeds no Form 1040 line
directly.

## Declarations

```catala-metadata
## The relationship of a person to the filers. DF: dependentsRelationship.xml
## /relationshipOptions (lines 5-35), one constructor per option, same order.
## DF derives /familyAndHousehold/*/relationship from a category question and
## one sub-question per category (lines 194-642); the result is always one
## of these options (`notRelated` gives `noneOfTheAbove`), so the derived
## fact is the input here.
declaration enumeration Relationship:
  -- BiologicalChild
  -- AdoptedChild
  -- StepChild
  -- FosterChild
  -- GrandChildOrOtherDescendantOfChild
  -- ChildInLaw
  -- Sibling
  -- ChildOfSibling
  -- HalfSibling
  -- ChildOfHalfSibling
  -- StepSibling
  -- ChildOfStepSibling
  -- OtherDescendantOfSibling
  -- OtherDescendantOfHalfSibling
  -- OtherDescendantOfStepSibling
  -- SiblingsSpouse
  -- SiblingInLaw
  -- Parent
  -- FosterParent
  -- SiblingOfParent
  -- GrandParent
  -- OtherAncestorOfParent
  -- StepParent
  -- ParentInLaw
  -- NoneOfTheAbove

## How long the person lived with the filer. DF: familyAndHousehold.xml
## /residencyDurationOptions (lines 34-44). The UI text of `sixToElevenMonths`
## is "More than 6 months (... days) but less than a year"
## (df-client-app/src/locales/en.yaml line 18028).
declaration enumeration ResidencyDuration:
  -- AllYear
  -- SixToElevenMonths
  -- LessThanSixMonths

## The `Writable` answers DF keeps per household member that the qualifying
## child tests read. Names, TIN, citizenship, CDCC and benefit-split answers
## belong to the modules that read them (#17, #18, #28).
declaration structure Person:
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/relationship
  data relationship content Relationship
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/dateOfBirth
  data date_of_birth content date
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/fullTimeStudent
  data full_time_student content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/permanentTotalDisability
  data permanent_total_disability content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/residencyDuration
  data residency_duration content ResidencyDuration
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/ownSupport
  data own_support content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/married
  data married content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableJointReturn
  data writable_joint_return content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableRequiredToFile
  data writable_required_to_file content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableFilingOnlyForRefund
  data writable_filing_only_for_refund content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableCouldBeQualifyingChildOfAnother
  data writable_could_be_qualifying_child_of_another content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writablePotentialClaimerMustFile
  data writable_potential_claimer_must_file content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writablePotentialClaimerDidFile
  data writable_potential_claimer_did_file content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writablePotentialClaimerFiledOnlyForRefund
  data writable_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund content boolean

## Ages used below. None is inflation indexed.
## IRC 152(c)(3)(A)(i): "has not attained the age of 19".
declaration qc_age_limit content integer equals 19
## IRC 152(c)(3)(A)(ii): "a student who has not attained the age of 24".
declaration qc_student_age_limit content integer equals 24
## DF product decision, familyAndHousehold.xml /studentStatusMayAffectBenefits
## (line 775): the student question is asked only of a person older than 18.
## A student of 18 or younger passes the age test without it.
declaration student_question_minimum_age content integer equals 18
## DF product decision, familyAndHousehold.xml
## /disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits (line 803): age 18 on July 2 of the
## tax year. No statute uses this number (quirk Q5).
declaration disability_question_age content integer equals 18
## DF /familyAndHousehold/*/ageAtFirstHalfOfYear (line 1585): the age on
## July 2, "the age of the dependent for the majority of the year".
declaration first_half_month content integer equals 7
declaration first_half_day content integer equals 2
```

## The scope for one person

```catala-metadata
declaration scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # One member of DF /familyAndHousehold
  input person content Person
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth (Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth)
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/age
  output age content integer
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/ageAtFirstHalfOfYear
  output age_at_first_half_of_year content integer
  # True if the person was born after the oldest filer (no DF fact; DF
  # writes the comparison inline in /ageTest and /isAStudentUnder24)
  output younger_than_oldest_filer condition
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/qcRelationshipTest
  output qc_relationship_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/studentStatusMayAffectBenefits
  output student_status_may_affect_benefits condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isAStudentUnder24
  output is_a_student_under24 condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits
  output disability_status_may_affect_benefits condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/ageTest
  output age_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/residencyTest
  output residency_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcSupportTest
  output qc_support_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/jointReturn
  output joint_return condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/requiredToFile
  output required_to_file condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/filingOnlyForRefund
  output filing_only_for_refund condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/jointReturnTest
  output joint_return_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild
  output qualifying_child condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcOfAnotherButNot
  output qc_of_another_but_not condition
  # Another taxpayer can also claim the person as a qualifying child
  # (IRC 152(c)(4) "2 or more taxpayers"; no DF fact)
  output other_taxpayer_can_claim_as_qualifying_child condition
  # The filer and another taxpayer can both claim the person, so the
  # 152(c)(4) tie-breaker is needed (DF-BUG-3; no DF fact)
  output ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker condition
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 152(c) Qualifying child

### (1) In general

IRC 152(c)(1) (`sources/statute/irc-152.html`): "The term "qualifying child"
means, with respect to any taxpayer for any taxable year, an individual— (A)
who bears a relationship to the taxpayer described in paragraph (2), (B) who
has the same principal place of abode as the taxpayer for more than one-half
of such taxable year, (C) who meets the age requirements of paragraph (3),
(D) who has not provided over one-half of such individual's own support for
the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins, and (E)
who has not filed a joint return (other than only for a claim of refund) with
the individual's spouse under section 6013 for the taxable year beginning in
the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins."

Pub. 501 (2024), `sources/pubs/p501--2024.pdf` p 11: "Qualifying Child ...
The five tests are: 1. Relationship, 2. Age, 3. Residency, 4. Support, 5.
Joint return." DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild`
(`familyAndHousehold.xml` line 2133): "Does the dependent pass all five
qualifying child tests?", the `All` of the five tests below.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # IRC 152(c)(1); Pub 501 p 11. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild
  rule qualifying_child under condition
    qc_relationship_test and age_test and residency_test
    and qc_support_test and joint_return_test
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(A) and (2) Relationship

IRC 152(c)(2): "an individual bears a relationship to the taxpayer described
in this paragraph if such individual is— (A) a child of the taxpayer or a
descendant of such a child, or (B) a brother, sister, stepbrother, or
stepsister of the taxpayer or a descendant of any such relative."

IRC 152(f)(1)(A): "The term "child" means an individual who is— (i) a son,
daughter, stepson, or stepdaughter of the taxpayer, or (ii) an eligible
foster child of the taxpayer." IRC 152(f)(1)(B): "a legally adopted
individual of the taxpayer, or an individual who is lawfully placed with the
taxpayer for legal adoption by the taxpayer, shall be treated as a child of
such individual by blood." IRC 152(f)(4): "The terms "brother" and "sister"
include a brother or sister by the half blood."

Pub. 501 p 12, Relationship Test: "To meet this test, a child must be: •
Your son, daughter, stepchild, or foster child, or a descendant (for
example, your grandchild) of any of them; or • Your brother, sister, half
brother, half sister, stepbrother, or stepsister, or a descendant (for
example, your niece or nephew) of any of them."

On a joint return the relationship may be to either spouse: Treas. Reg.
§ 1.152-2(d) (`sources/regs/cfr-1.152-2.xml`): "In the case of a joint return
it is not necessary that the prescribed relationship exist between the person
claimed as a dependent and the spouse who furnishes the support; it is
sufficient if the prescribed relationship exists with respect to either
spouse." DF asks one relationship "to the taxpayer(s)" (`relationship`,
`dependentsRelationship.xml` line 196), so this is the answer's meaning.

DF `qcRelationshipTest` (`dependentsRelationship.xml` line 986) lists the 14
options below: the five child options (descendants of a child are
`grandChildOrOtherDescendantOfChild`) and the nine sibling options. In-laws,
parents, ancestors, siblings of a parent and unrelated persons fail.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # IRC 152(c)(2), 152(f)(1), 152(f)(4); Pub 501 p 12.
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/qcRelationshipTest
  rule qc_relationship_test under condition
    match person.relationship with pattern
    -- BiologicalChild: true
    -- AdoptedChild: true
    -- StepChild: true
    -- FosterChild: true
    -- GrandChildOrOtherDescendantOfChild: true
    -- Sibling: true
    -- HalfSibling: true
    -- StepSibling: true
    -- ChildOfSibling: true
    -- ChildOfHalfSibling: true
    -- ChildOfStepSibling: true
    -- OtherDescendantOfSibling: true
    -- OtherDescendantOfHalfSibling: true
    -- OtherDescendantOfStepSibling: true
    -- anything: false
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(C) and (3) Age

IRC 152(c)(3)(A): "an individual meets the requirements of this paragraph if
such individual is younger than the taxpayer claiming such individual as a
qualifying child and— (i) has not attained the age of 19 as of the close of
the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins, or (ii)
is a student who has not attained the age of 24 as of the close of such
calendar year." IRC 152(c)(3)(B): "In the case of an individual who is
permanently and totally disabled (as defined in section 22(e)(3)) at any time
during such calendar year, the requirements of subparagraph (A) shall be
treated as met with respect to such individual."

IRC 152(f)(2): "The term "student" means an individual who during each of 5
calendar months during the calendar year in which the taxable year of the
taxpayer begins— (A) is a full-time student at an educational organization
described in section 170(b)(1)(A)(ii), or (B) is pursuing a full-time course
of institutional on-farm training". DF asks one question, `fullTimeStudent`
("Is the dependent a full-time student?").

Pub. 501 p 12, Age Test: "To meet this test, a child must be: • Under age 19
at the end of the year and younger than you (or your spouse if filing
jointly); • A student under age 24 at the end of the year and younger than
you (or your spouse if filing jointly); or • Permanently and totally disabled
at any time during the year, regardless of age." Pub. 501 p 13: "if you are
married filing jointly, the child must be younger than you or your spouse but
doesn't have to be younger than both of you." That is why DF compares with
the oldest filer's date of birth (`/minimumFilerDateOfBirth`).

#### Age at the end of the year

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/age` (line 758) is the tax year minus the year of
birth. A person attains an age on the anniversary of the birth, so this is
exactly the age attained "as of the close of the calendar year": Pub. 501
p 12, "Your child turned 19 on December 10. ... this child doesn't meet the
age test because, at the end of the year, this child wasn't under age 19."

`/familyAndHousehold/*/ageAtFirstHalfOfYear` (line 1585) is the age on
July 2: one less than `age` if the birthday falls after July 2.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # Age at the close of the tax year. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/age
  definition age equals F.tax_year - Date.get_year of person.date_of_birth

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/ageAtFirstHalfOfYear (age on July 2)
  definition age_at_first_half_of_year equals
    if Date.get_month of person.date_of_birth > first_half_month
       or (Date.get_month of person.date_of_birth = first_half_month
           and Date.get_day of person.date_of_birth > first_half_day)
    then age - 1
    else age

  # IRC 152(c)(3)(A) "younger than the taxpayer"; Pub 501 p 13.
  # DF: the comparison dateOfBirth > /minimumFilerDateOfBirth written inline
  # in /familyAndHousehold/*/ageTest (line 1840) and /isAStudentUnder24 (line 4741)
  rule younger_than_oldest_filer under condition
    person.date_of_birth > minimum_filer_date_of_birth
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### Student under 24

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/studentStatusMayAffectBenefits` (line 775) is true
for ages 19 to 23 at the end of the year; `/isAStudentUnder24` (line 4731)
is a full-time student of that age who is younger than the oldest filer. A
student of 18 or younger already passes under 152(c)(3)(A)(i), so the
age floor changes nothing.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/studentStatusMayAffectBenefits
  rule student_status_may_affect_benefits under condition
    age > student_question_minimum_age and age < qc_student_age_limit
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(3)(A)(ii), 152(f)(2). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isAStudentUnder24
  rule is_a_student_under24 under condition
    person.full_time_student and student_status_may_affect_benefits
    and younger_than_oldest_filer
  consequence fulfilled
```

#### Permanently and totally disabled

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits` (line 803) is
`Any(ageAtFirstHalfOfYear >= 18, All(ageAtFirstHalfOfYear < 18, dateOfBirth
< /minimumFilerDateOfBirth))`, and `/ageTest` ANDs it with
`permanentTotalDisability`.

**Is the condition inverted? No** (scope.md section 11 item 5, quirk Q5,
re-read for issue #16). The description says the question is asked when the
person "is not 18.5, but they are younger than the filer". The code tests
`dateOfBirth < /minimumFilerDateOfBirth`: born *before* the oldest filer,
i.e. *older* than every filer. The code is right and the description is
wrong. A person under 18 on July 2 is at most 18 at the end of the year, so
if they are also younger than the oldest filer they pass 152(c)(3)(A)(i)
whatever the disability answer; disability can only change the age test for
someone 18 or older on July 2 or not younger than the oldest filer, which is
the set the code selects. The literal description (`>`) would ask exactly
the people for whom the answer cannot matter, and would ignore it for a
disabled person under 18 who is older than a young filer (for example a
disabled 17-year-old sibling of a 16-year-old filer), who passes under
152(c)(3)(B).

The gate still misses one case. A disabled person under 18 on July 2 born on
exactly the same day as the oldest filer (a twin of the filer) is neither
younger (`dateOfBirth > min`) nor older (`dateOfBirth < min`), so DF fails
the age test, while 152(c)(3)(B) says the age requirements "shall be treated
as met". Following CONVENTIONS section 8 (statute wins), `age_test` below
counts the disability answer for everyone, as the statute does. The gate is
kept as an output because DF also uses it for the benefit-split rules (#18)
and Schedule EIC line 4b.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # Not inverted (Q5): the gate selects the persons for whom the disability
  # answer can change the age test (text above).
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits
  rule disability_status_may_affect_benefits under condition
    age_at_first_half_of_year >= disability_question_age
    or (age_at_first_half_of_year < disability_question_age
        and person.date_of_birth < minimum_filer_date_of_birth)
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(11): DF ANDs the disability answer with
  # disabilityStatusMayAffectBenefits, which fails a disabled person under 18
  # born on the same day as the oldest filer. IRC 152(c)(3)(B): for a person
  # "permanently and totally disabled ... at any time during such calendar
  # year, the requirements of subparagraph (A) shall be treated as met";
  # Pub 501 p 12: "regardless of age". Status: candidate. We follow the law.
  # IRC 152(c)(3); Pub 501 p 12. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/ageTest
  rule age_test under condition
    (age < qc_age_limit and younger_than_oldest_filer)
    or is_a_student_under24
    or person.permanent_total_disability
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(B) Residency

IRC 152(c)(1)(B): "who has the same principal place of abode as the taxpayer
for more than one-half of such taxable year". Pub. 501 p 13, Residency Test:
"To meet this test, your child must have lived with you for more than half
the year. There are exceptions for temporary absences, children who were
born or died during the year, adopted or foster children, kidnapped
children, and children of divorced or separated parents."

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/residencyTest` (line 1869): the answer is not
`lessThanSixMonths`. The exceptions are folded into the answer by the
questionnaire (temporary absences count as time lived together); the
divorced-parents rule is issue #18.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # IRC 152(c)(1)(B); Pub 501 p 13. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/residencyTest
  rule residency_test under condition
    person.residency_duration != LessThanSixMonths
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(D) Support

IRC 152(c)(1)(D): "who has not provided over one-half of such individual's
own support for the calendar year". Pub. 501 p 14, Support Test (To Be a
Qualifying Child): "To meet this test, the child can't have provided more
than half of the child's own support for the year." DF
`/familyAndHousehold/*/qcSupportTest` (line 2696) is `Not(ownSupport)`, where
`ownSupport` asks "Did the dependent provide more than half their own
support?". The 152(f)(5) scholarship rule is part of answering that
question.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # IRC 152(c)(1)(D); Pub 501 p 14. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcSupportTest
  rule qc_support_test under condition not person.own_support
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(E) Joint return

IRC 152(c)(1)(E): "who has not filed a joint return (other than only for a
claim of refund) with the individual's spouse under section 6013". Pub. 501
p 15, Joint Return Test (To Be a Qualifying Child): "To meet this test, the
child can't file a joint return for the year. Exception. An exception to the
joint return test applies if your child and their spouse file a joint return
only to claim a refund of income tax withheld or estimated tax paid." Pub.
501 p 15, Example 2: the couple "file a joint return only to get a refund of
the withheld taxes. The exception to the joint return test applies, so this
child may be your qualifying child if all the other tests are met."

DF asks three questions of a married person: are they filing jointly, are
they required to file, are they filing only for a refund. Each derived fact
is ANDed with `married` (lines 2027-2096). `/filingOnlyForRefund` is also
ANDed with "`requiredToFile` is complete and false"; with complete inputs
that is `not required_to_file`. `/jointReturnTest` (line 2098) passes if the
person is not married, does not file jointly, or files only for a refund and
is not required to file.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/jointReturn
  rule joint_return under condition
    person.married and person.writable_joint_return
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/requiredToFile
  rule required_to_file under condition
    person.married and joint_return and person.writable_required_to_file
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/filingOnlyForRefund (IsComplete(requiredToFile)
  # is always true here, CONVENTIONS section 3)
  rule filing_only_for_refund under condition
    person.married and joint_return and person.writable_filing_only_for_refund
    and not required_to_file
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(1)(E); Pub 501 p 15. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/jointReturnTest
  rule joint_return_test under condition
    (not person.married)
    or (not joint_return)
    or (filing_only_for_refund and not required_to_file)
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (4) Two or more taxpayers who can claim the same child

IRC 152(c)(4)(A): "if (but for this paragraph) an individual may be claimed
as a qualifying child by 2 or more taxpayers for a taxable year beginning in
the same calendar year, such individual shall be treated as the qualifying
child of the taxpayer who is— (i) a parent of the individual, or (ii) if
clause (i) does not apply, the taxpayer with the highest adjusted gross
income for such taxable year." IRC 152(c)(4)(B): "If the parents claiming
any qualifying child do not file a joint return together, such child shall
be treated as the qualifying child of— (i) the parent with whom the child
resided for the longest period of time during the taxable year, or (ii) if
the child resides with both parents for the same amount of time during such
taxable year, the parent with the highest adjusted gross income." IRC
152(c)(4)(C): "If the parents of an individual may claim such individual as
a qualifying child but no parent so claims the individual, such individual
may be claimed as the qualifying child of another taxpayer but only if the
adjusted gross income of such taxpayer is higher than the highest adjusted
gross income of any parent of the individual."

Pub. 501 p 15, Qualifying Child of More Than One Person: "If your qualifying
child isn't a qualifying child of anyone else, this topic doesn't apply to
you". Pub. 501 p 18, Not a Qualifying Child Test: "A child isn't the
qualifying child of any other taxpayer ... if the child's parent (or other
person for whom the child is defined as a qualifying child) isn't required
to file an income tax return and either: • Doesn't file an income tax
return, or • Files a return only to get a refund of income tax withheld or
estimated tax paid."

What DF does (DF-BUG-3, `docs/direct-file-bugs.md`): nothing. It asks
whether the person "could be the qualifying child of another taxpayer"
(`writableCouldBeQualifyingChildOfAnother`, line 2450) and whether that
taxpayer must file, did file, or filed only for a refund (lines 2483-2547),
but uses the answers only for the qualifying-relative path (#17) and for the
EITC, where `/familyAndHousehold/*/eitcQualifyingChild` carries "TODO: take
into account QC of another" (line 2259). A filer whose child is also the
qualifying child of a taxpayer with priority under 152(c)(4) still gets the
child for every benefit.

What we do: knock out. Deciding 152(c)(4) needs facts about the other
taxpayer that no DF question collects (whether they are a parent, the
child's time with each parent, both AGIs, whether a parent claims the child),
so the rule cannot be computed from the return. The knockout fires when the
person passes the five tests for the filer and the other taxpayer is a real
competing claimant: they "could" claim the child and are not the
non-filer / refund-only case of Pub. 501 p 18 (DF's own
`/familyAndHousehold/*/qcOfAnotherButNot`, line 2549, with its inputs
complete). The filer's spouse on a joint return is not "another taxpayer"
(Pub. 501 p 15: "except your spouse with whom you plan to file a joint
return"); DF's question is understood the same way.

```catala
scope QualifyingChildTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcOfAnotherButNot (inputs complete, so the
  # derived potentialClaimer* facts reduce to the answers below)
  rule qc_of_another_but_not under condition
    person.writable_could_be_qualifying_child_of_another
    and not person.writable_potential_claimer_must_file
    and ((not person.writable_potential_claimer_did_file)
         or person.writable_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund)
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(4); Pub 501 pp 15, 18. No DF fact (see the text above).
  rule other_taxpayer_can_claim_as_qualifying_child under condition
    person.writable_could_be_qualifying_child_of_another
    and not qc_of_another_but_not
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(3): DF never applies IRC 152(c)(4). We cannot apply it from the
  # facts on the return, so the return is refused when it would matter.
  # Status: candidate (docs/direct-file-bugs.md), owner #16, #60.
  rule ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker under condition
    qualifying_child and other_taxpayer_can_claim_as_qualifying_child
  consequence fulfilled
```

## The household: every person

DF evaluates each `/familyAndHousehold/*/` fact per collection member. The
scope `QualifyingChildren` does the same with `map each`, and counts the
members who are qualifying children (no DF fact; a convenience output for
tests and later modules).

```catala-metadata
declaration scope QualifyingChildren:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold
  input persons content list of Person
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/... (one result per person, same order)
  output results content list of QualifyingChildTest
  # Number of persons with `qualifying_child` (no DF fact)
  output qualifying_child_count content integer

  # Knockouts, CONVENTIONS section 6
  # Some person needs the IRC 152(c)(4) tie-breaker (DF-BUG-3; no DF fact)
  output ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker condition
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope QualifyingChildren:
  definition results equals
    map each p among persons to
      (output of QualifyingChildTest with {
         -- person: p
         -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth })

  definition qualifying_child_count equals
    number of (list of r among results such that r.qualifying_child)

  # DF-BUG(3): IRC 152(c)(4). No DF fact (DF has no knockout here).
  rule ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker under condition
    exists r among results such that r.ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker
  consequence fulfilled

  rule unsupported under condition
    ko_qualifying_child_tie_breaker
  consequence fulfilled
```
